{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: To evaluate the relative risk (RR) of serious and non-serious adverse events in patients treated with exercise therapy compared with those in a non-exercising control group. DESIGN: Systematic review and meta-analysis. DATA SOURCES: Primary studies were identified based on The Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews investigating the effect of exercise therapy. ELIGIBILITY CRITERIA: At least two of the authors independently evaluated all identified reviews and primary studies. Randomised controlled trials were included if they compared any exercise therapy intervention with a non-exercising control. Two authors independently extracted data. The RR of serious and non-serious adverse events was estimated separately. RESULTS: 180 Cochrane reviews were included and from these, 773 primary studies were identified. Of these, 378 studies (n=38 368 participants) reported serious adverse events and 375 studies (n=38 517 participants) reported non-serious adverse events. We found no increase in risk of serious adverse events (RR=0.96 (95%CI 0.90 to 1.02, I2: 0.0%) due to exercise therapy. There was, however, an increase in non-serious adverse events (RR=1.19 (95%CI 1.09 to 1.30, I2: 0.0%). The number needed to treat for an additional harmful outcome for non-serious adverse events was 6 [95%CI 4 to 11). CONCLUSION: Participating in an exercise intervention increased the relative risk of non-serious adverse events, but not of serious adverse events. Exercise therapy may therefore be recommended as a relatively safe intervention.PROSPERO registration numberCRD42014014819.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Niemeijer, Andre","Lund, Hans","Stafne, Signe Nilssen","Ipsen, Thomas","Goldschmidt, Cathrine Luhaäär","Jørgensen, Claus Thomas","Juhl, Carsten Bogh"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-589940"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Norway"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","St. Olav University Hospital"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"adverse events of exercise therapy in randomised controlled trials a systematic review and meta analysis","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/169080786/Niemeijer2.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-589940","identifier":[{"id":"85072824055","type":"scopus"},{"id":"31563884","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["sdu-3a69c7d2-ad51-4d84-b924-bb3ce86b7895"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-9C923F90CC3BC095951C800A7924BF11"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/169080786/Niemeijer2.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2020","yearSub":"2020"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"no","key":"st olav university hospital","loc":["Norway","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"St. Olav University Hospital","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Andre","key":"niemeijerpau","last":"Niemeijer","locid":"f3858985-aa8f-47e2-8e5d-afdd9dd78ed9","name":"Niemeijer, Andre","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Ha