{"author_count":1,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Carsten René"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-129731"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the psychotherapeutic stance","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"db","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-129731","ids":["au-00ceed4d-047c-481e-9b13-d3f537ea1c21"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-D2DAE657C978DFCB13E10A88A20E206D"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Department of Psychology and Behavioural Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Carsten René","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"3763a4db-6ce1-4e54-ac67-524a9604e1ce","name":"Jørgensen, Carsten René","orcid":"0000-0002-7324-1628","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1007/978-3-030-20437-2","eisbn":["9783030204372"],"isbn":["9783030204365"],"pages":"290","publisher":"Springer","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-00ceed4d-047c-481e-9b13-d3f537ea1c21"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"The psychotherapeutic stance","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-129731","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Graft-versus-host disease (GVHD) is a main cause of morbidity and mortality following hematopoietic stem cell transplantation. The cumulative incidence of acute and chronic GVHD (aGVHD, cGVHD) reaches 30%-50% and 20% in pediatric populations, respectively. Prednisolone and/or calcineurin inhibitors (CNI) are first-line treatments, but no superior second-line treatment has yet been established. Several treatments have been suggested, among others extracorporeal photopheresis (ECP). Technical advances have made treatment of pediatric patients possible; however, only few reports on the feasibility of ECP in children have been published. We retrospectively studied the feasibility, safety, and efficacy of ECP in 15 children with steroid-dependent/refractory acute or chronic GVHD, who initiated ECP treatment between April 2014 and January 2018. Only few and mild side effects directly related to the ECP procedure were registered, even in patients with low body weight. The most frequent cause of shortened or canceled ECP treatment was difficulties with vascular accesses, which were more rarely seen using central venous catheters with larger lumens and made of stiffer material. Nine patients had grade II-III aGVHD. Six of these experienced a response to ECP at day 28, while eight of nine had responded at the last ECP treatment. Six patients had cGVHD when ECP was initiated, and of these, four had a partial response at last ECP treatment. We found ECP to be a feasible and safe treatment, and particularly, children with aGVHD appeared to respond well to ECP.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Winther-Jørgensen, Signe","Nygaard, Marietta","Heilmann, Carsten","Ifversen, Marianne","Sørensen, Kaspar","Müller, Klaus","Masmas, Tania"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-478562"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["ECP","Feasibility","GvHD","Pediatric"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"feasibility of extracorporeal photopheresis in pediatric patients with graft versus host disease after hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-478562","identifier":[{"id":"85064524876","type":"scopus"},{"id":"30973668","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-8e5d9b03-ab2d-43cc-9443-2d91567f55ef"],"keyword":["ECP","GVHD","HCT","feasibility","pediatric"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-C85E84C7FFC328D9A335F36A87F81AA4"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Paediatrics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Signe","key":"wintherjørgensenpau","last":"Winther-Jørgensen","name":"Winther-Jørgensen, Signe","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Marietta","key":"nygaardpau","last":"Nygaard","name":"Nygaard, Marietta","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Carsten","key":"heilmannpau","last":"Heilmann","name":"Heilmann, Carsten","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Marianne","key":"ifversenpau","last":"Ifversen","name":"Ifversen, Marianne","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Kaspar","key":"sørensenpau","last":"Sørensen","name":"Sørensen, Kaspar","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Klaus","key":"müllerpau","last":"Müller","locid":"b909f13e-79e3-4363-aaff-e5a5ecbdf7ce","name":"Müller, Klaus","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Tania","key":"masmaspau","last":"Masmas","name":"Masmas, Tania","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["30973668"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/petr.13416","issn":["13973142","13982265"],"issn_org":["13973142"],"issue":"4","journal":"Pediatric Transplantation","paperid":"e13416","status":"p","title":"Pediatric Transplantation","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85064524876"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-8e5d9b03-ab2d-43cc-9443-2d91567f55ef"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Feasibility of extracorporeal photopheresis in pediatric patients with graft-versus-host disease after hematopoietic stem cell transplantation","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-478562","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Background: Serious mental illness (SMI) reduces life expectancy, primarily due to somatic comorbidity linked to obesity. Meta-analyses have found beneficial effects of lifestyle interventions in people with SMI and recommended their implementation to manage obesity. Objective: The objective of this systematic review was to assess the benefits and harms of individualized lifestyle interventions for weight in people diagnosed with SMI and to explore potential mediators and moderators of the effect. Methods: The protocol was registered at PROSPERO (CRD42016049093). Randomized clinical trials (RCTs) assessing the effect of individualized lifestyle interventions on weight management in people with SMI were included. Primary outcomes were differences in endpoint body mass index (BMI) and the proportion achieving clinically relevant weight loss (≥5%). Secondary outcomes included quality of life, cardiometabolic risk factors, and adverse effects. Results: We included 41 RCTs (n = 4,267). All trials were at high risk of bias according to the Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions. The experimental interventions reduced the mean difference in BMI by -0.63 kg/m (95% confidence interval [CI] = -1.02 to -0.23; p = 0.002; I = 70.7%) compared to the control groups. At postintervention follow-up (17 RCTs), the effect size remained similar but was no longer significant (BMI = -0.63 kg/m 95% CI = -1.30 to 0.04; p = 0.07; I = 48.8%). The risk ratio for losing ≥5% of baseline weight was 1.51 (95% CI = 1.07-2.13; p = 0.02) compared to the control groups. GRADE showed very low or low quality of evidence. Conclusion: There is a statistically significant, but clinically insignificant, mean effect of individualized lifestyle interventions for weight reduction in people with SMI.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Speyer, Helene","Jakobsen, Ane Storch","Westergaard, Casper","Nørgaard, Hans Christian Brix","Pisinger, Charlotta","Krogh, Jesper","Hjorthøj, Carsten","Nordentoft, Merete","Gluud, Christian","Correll, Christoph U.","Jørgensen, Kirstine Bro"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-028976"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Speyer, Helene"],"country":["Germany","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Charité-Universitätsmedizin Berlin","Hofstra University","Zucker Hillside Hospital"],"keyword":["Adverse effect","Antipsychotics","Lifestyle intervention","Metabolic risk factors","Obesity","Quality of Life","Serious mental illness","Weight loss"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"lifestyle interventions for weight management in people with serious mental illness a systematic review with meta analysis trial sequential analysis and meta regression analysis exploring the mediators and moderators of treatment effects","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85072542610","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-028976","identifier":[{"id":"85072542610","type":"scopus"},{"id":"31522170","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-e0dca856-d8ff-46f4-93e9-1fb13584faca","ku-d53c4798-89d9-43a5-8d3b-95e581f59aa3"],"keyword":["Adverse effect","Antipsychotics","Lifestyle intervention","Metabolic risk factors","Obesity","Quality of life","Serious mental illness","Weight loss"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-2C050E8C9047B697DFEA5342A3C5795F"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Psychosis Research Unit"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Section of Social Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"zucker hillside hospital","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Zucker Hillside Hospital","used":1},{"affno":"5","country":"us","key":"hofstra university","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Hofstra University","used":1},{"affno":"6","country":"de","key":"charité universitätsmedizin berlin","loc":["Germany","Europe, EU","OECD"],"name":"Charité-Universitätsmedizin Berlin","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Helene","key":"speyerpau","last":"Speyer","name":"Speyer, Helene","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Ane Storch","key":"jakobsenpau","last":"Jakobsen","name":"Jakobsen, Ane Storch","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Casper","key":"westergaardpau","last":"Westergaard","name":"Westergaard, Casper","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hans Christian Brix","key":"nørgaardpau","last":"Nørgaard","locid":"5a728e47-29dc-4841-996b-591884d776c9","name":"Nørgaard, Hans Christian Brix","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","first":"Charlotta","key":"pisingerpau","last":"Pisinger","locid":"39c83c02-764d-46f9-935c-ab511b412204","name":"Pisinger, Charlotta","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jesper","key":"kroghpau","last":"Krogh","name":"Krogh, Jesper","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Carsten","key":"hjorthøjpau","last":"Hjorthøj","locid":"d4b636e3-7c21-48a0-b812-de219ca78a9e","name":"Hjorthøj, Carsten","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Merete","key":"nordentoftpau","last":"Nordentoft","locid":"6d0fe19c-7c81-463b-8bc8-822d71584b69","name":"Nordentoft, Merete","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Christian","key":"gluudpau","last":"Gluud","name":"Gluud, Christian","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4 5 6","corresponding":null,"first":"Christoph U.","key":"correllpau","last":"Correll","name":"Correll, Christoph U.","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Kirstine Bro","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, Kirstine Bro","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31522170"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1159/000502293","issn":["00333190","14230348"],"issn_org":["00333190"],"issue":"6","journal":"Psychotherapy and Psychosomatics","pages":"350-362","publisher":"S. Karger AG","status":"p","title":"Psychotherapy and Psychosomatics","volume":"88"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85072542610"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-e0dca856-d8ff-46f4-93e9-1fb13584faca","ku-d53c4798-89d9-43a5-8d3b-95e581f59aa3"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Lifestyle Interventions for Weight Management in People with Serious Mental Illness : A Systematic Review with Meta-Analysis, Trial Sequential Analysis, and Meta-Regression Analysis Exploring the Mediators and Moderators of Treatment Effects","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-028976","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Oral delivery of proteins and peptides is one of the main challenges in pharmaceutical drug development. Microdevices have the possibility to protect the therapeutics until release is desired, avoiding losses by degradation. One type of microdevice is polymeric microcontainers. In this study, lysozyme is chosen as model protein and loaded into microcontainers with the permeation enhancer sodium decanoate (C10). The loaded microcontainers are sealed and functionalized by applying polymeric lids onto the cavity of the devices. The first lid is poly(lactic-co-glycolic) acid (PLGA) and on top of this either polyethylene glycol (PEG) or chitosan is applied (PLGA+PEG or PLGA+chitosan, respectively). The functionalization is evaluated in vitro for morphology, drug release, and mucoadhesive properties. These are coupled with in vitro and ex vivo studies using Caco-2 cells, Caco-2/HT29-MTX-E12 co-cultures, and porcine intestinal tissue. PLGA+chitosan shows slower release compared to PLGA+PEG or only PLGA in buffer and the transport of lysozyme across cell cultures is not enhanced compared to the bulk powder. Microcontainers coated with chitosan or PEG demonstrate a three times stronger adhesion during ex vivo mucoadhesion studies compared to samples without coatings. Altogether, functionalized microcontainers with mucoadhesive properties and tunable release for oral protein delivery are developed and characterized.","author_count":10,"contributors":["Mazzoni, Chiara","Jacobsen, Rasmus Due","Mortensen, Jacob","Jørgensen, Jacob Rune","Vaut, Lukas","Jacobsen, Jette","Gundlach, Carsten","Müllertz, Anette","Nielsen, Line Hagner","Boisen, Anja"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-033656"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Mazzoni, Chiara"],"keyword":["Microdevices","Mucoadhesion","Oral drug delivery","Protein","polymeric coating"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"