{"abstract":"Donald Trump - en klyngebombe af mistillid og splittelse","author_count":1,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Carsten René"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-272928"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"level":"pop","mra":["soc"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"und","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"trump gør os skøre","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dna","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-272928","ids":["au-fbb367fd-a2df-459d-88f5-79d733c55e0a"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Popular","mra":["Social Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-2B0B888F1DEEF656F3A87B6723BF14BD"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Aarhus BSS, Department of Psychology and Behavioural Sciences"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Carsten René","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"3763a4db-6ce1-4e54-ac67-524a9604e1ce","name":"Jørgensen, Carsten René","orcid":"0000-0002-7324-1628","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["16022572"],"newspaper":"Information","pages":"16","publisher":"Dagbladet Information","status":"p","title":"Information","volume":"Sektion 1: Debat"},"review":"Undetermined","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-fbb367fd-a2df-459d-88f5-79d733c55e0a"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Trump gør os skøre","type":"Newspaper Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-272928","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) reconstruction, using hamstring auto-graft is a common surgical procedure, which often leads to persistent hamstring muscle-strength deficiency and reduced function. The purpose of this randomized controlled trial (RCT) is to investigate the effect of a combined, progressive, strength and neuromuscular exercise intervention on knee muscle strength, functional capacity and hamstring muscle-tendon morphology in ACL-reconstructed patients with persistent hamstring muscle-strength deficiency compared with controls. METHODS/DESIGN: The study is designed as a multicenter, parallel-group RCT with balanced randomization (1:1) and blinded outcome assessments (level of evidence: II) and will be reported in accordance with the CONSORT Statement. Fifty ACL-reconstructed patients (hamstring auto-graft) with persistent limb-to-limb knee-flexor muscle-strength asymmetry at 12-24 months' post surgery, will be recruited through outpatient clinics and advertisements. Patients will be randomized to a 12-week progressive, strength and neuromuscular exercise group (SNG) with supervised training twice weekly or a control intervention (CON) consisting of a home-based, low-intensity exercise program. Outcome measures include between-group change in maximal isometric knee-flexor strength (primary outcome) and knee-extensor muscle strength, hamstring-to-quadriceps strength ratios of the leg that has been operated on and Knee injury and Osteoarthritis Outcome Score (KOOS) (secondary outcomes). In addition, several explorative outcomes will be investigated: The International Knee Documentation Committee Subjective Knee Form (IKDC), the Tegner Activity Score, rate of force development (RFD) for the knee flexors and extensors, tendon regeneration and potential muscle hypertrophy at graft harvest site evaluated by magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), postural control, kinetic/kinematic gait characteristics and knee-related functional capacity. DISCUSSION: This RCT is designed to investigate the effect of combined, progressive-resistance and neuromuscular exercises on knee-flexor/extensor strength, in the late rehabilitation phase following ACL reconstruction. Reduced hamstring strength represents a potential risk factor for secondary ACL rupture and accelerated progression of osteoarthritis. If deemed effective, the intervention paradigm introduced in this study may help to improve current treatment strategies in ACL-reconstructed patients. TRIAL REGISTRATION: ClinicalTrials.gov, ID: NCT02939677 (recruiting). Registered on 20 October 2016.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Bregenhof, Bo","Jørgensen, Uffe","Aagaard, Per","Nissen, Nis","Creaby, Mark W","Thorlund, Jonas Bloch","Jensen, Carsten","Torfing, Trine","Holsgaard-Larsen, Anders"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-639714"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["OECD","Oceania"],"country":["Australia"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Australian Catholic University"],"keyword":["ACL reconstruction","Exercise","Muscle Strength","Physical function","Rehabilitation"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the effect of targeted exercise on knee muscle function in patients with persistent hamstring deficiency following acl reconstruction study protocol for a randomized controlled trial","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13063-018-2448-3","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-639714","identifier":[{"id":"85041070343","type":"scopus"},{"id":"29373984","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC5787267","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["sdu-e849945f-17c8-44e5-99e0-ff1c33f598f5"],"keyword":["ACL reconstruction","Exercise","Muscle strength","Physical function","Rehabilitation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-32CD2D972D7588B808094A64EF4A628E"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/135387038/The_effect_of_targeted_exercise_on_knee_muscle_function_in_patients_with_persistent_hamstring_deficiency_following_ACL_reconstruction.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13063-018-2448-3","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of orthopaedic surgery (Odense)","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Lillebælt Hospital, Research Unit of Orthopaedics (Kolding)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"au","key":"australian catholic university","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Australian Catholic University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Bo","key":"bregenhofpau","last":"Bregenhof","locid":"2697409c-2f98-40b4-9bd7-6fe3828131e7","name":"Bregenhof, Bo","orcid":"0000-0002-4363-5335","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Uffe","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"bac1dfd1-fa52-49a1-bf89-a932c304da67","name":"Jørgensen, Uffe","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Per","key":"aagaardpau","last":"Aagaard","locid":"8452f86e-3c45-4ecb-9e11-54c72d45d669","name":"Aagaard, Per","orcid":"0000-0002-9773-7361","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Nis","key":"nissenpau","last":"Nissen","locid":"c7eeda48-f8da-46b2-a951-69698598bc9f","name":"Nissen, Nis","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Mark W","key":"creabypau","last":"Creaby","name":"Creaby, Mark W","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jonas Bloch","key":"thorlundpau","last":"Thorlund","locid":"5b59bfe4-8aff-41d8-8b2d-4b19ab3e56c4","name":"Thorlund, Jonas Bloch","orcid":"0000-0001-7789-8224","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Carsten","key":"jensenpau","last":"Jensen","locid":"8561dcdc-4528-4cdd-9c95-b54116e10520","name":"Jensen, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0002-8074-1583","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Trine","key":"torfingpau","last":"Torfing","locid":"12fcde25-9d61-472d-beed-78e5897f877e","name":"Torfing, Trine","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders","key":"holsgaardlarsenpau","last":"Holsgaard-Larsen","locid":"3c1cf5a4-81b1-49f3-ad79-d086b74b1561","name":"Holsgaard-Larsen, Anders","orcid":"0000-0003-4106-6930","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["29373984"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1186/s13063-018-2448-3","issn":["14686708","17456215"],"issn_org":["17456215"],"journal":"Trials","paperid":"75","publisher":"BioMed Central","status":"p","title":"Trials","volume":"19"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85041070343"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-e849945f-17c8-44e5-99e0-ff1c33f598f5"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"The effect of targeted exercise on knee-muscle function in patients with persistent hamstring deficiency following ACL reconstruction : study protocol for a randomized controlled trial","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-639714","year":"2018","yearsub":"2018"}
{"abstract":"Zoonotic infections transmitted from terrestrial and marine mammals to humans in European Arctic are of unknown significance, despite considerable potential for transmission due to local hunt and a rapidly changing environment. As an example, infection with Brucella bacteria may have significant impact on human health due to consumption of raw meat or otherwise contact with tissues and fluids of infected game species such as muskoxen and polar bears. Here, we present serological results for Baffin Bay polar bears (Ursus maritimus) (n = 96) and North East Greenland muskoxen (Ovibos moschatus) (n = 32) for antibodies against Brucella spp. The analysis was a two-step trial initially using the Rose Bengal Test (RBT), followed by confirmative competitive enzyme-linked immunosorbent assays of RBT-positive samples. No muskoxen had antibodies against Brucella spp., while antibodies were detected in six polar bears (6.25%) rendering a seroprevalence in line with previous findings in other Arctic regions. Seropositivity was not related to sex, age or biometrics i.e. size and body condition. Whether Brucella spp. antibodies found in polar bears were due to either prey spill over or true recurrent Brucella spp. infections is unknown. Our results therefore highlight the importance of further research into the zoonotic aspects of Brucella spp. infections, and the impact on wildlife and human health in the Arctic region.","author_count":22,"contributors":["Sonne, Christian","Andersen-Ranberg, Emilie Ulrikka","Rajala, Elisabeth L.","Agerholm, Jørgen S.","Bonefeld-Jørgensen, Eva Cecilie","Desforges, Jean-Pierre","Eulaers, Igor","Gustavson, Kim","Jenssen, Bjørn Munro","Koch, Anders","Rosing-Asvid, Aqqalu","Schmidt, Niels Martin","Grøndahl, Carsten","Mosbacher, Jesper Bruun","Siebert, Ursula","Tryland, Morten","Mulvad, Gert","Born, Erik W.","Laidre, Kristin","Wiig, Øystein","Dietz, Rune","Magnusson, Ulf"],"cuid":["S02-088.24-029453"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Germany","Greenland","Norway","Svalbard and Jan Mayen","Sweden","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Greenland Institute of Natural Resources","Norwegian University of Science and Technology","Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences","University Centre in Svalbard","University of Greenland","University of Oslo","University of Tromsø – The Arctic University of Norway","University of Veterinary Medicine Hannover, Foundation","University of Washington"],"keyword":["Arctic","Humans","One Health","Zoonosis"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"GV":["DI-GV-ALL","DI-GV-SSI"],"OT":["Copenhagen Zoo"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-GV-SSI","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"prevalence of antibodies against i brucella spp i in west greenland polar bears i ursus maritimus i and east greenland muskoxen i ovibos moschatus i","src":["au","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85044097852","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-088.24-029453","identifier":[{"id":"85044097852","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-e7a9973e-7207-4086-9c55-0f8daec22f36","ku-c0f2a601-7601-4196-ad59-1d14dce49e91"],"keyword":["Arctic","Humans","One health","Zoonosis"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-A23BFC79EA3A87CA39E513560E3DF884"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2018","yearSub":"2018"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus Un