{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: The use of low-dose aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid [ASA]) has been suggested to attenuate growth of abdominal aortic aneurysms (AAAs), yet solid clinical evidence of this hypothesis is still missing. This study aimed to investigate whether preadmission ASA use influenced the risk of presenting with rupture of AAA (rAAA) on hospital admission and subsequent 30-day case fatality. METHODS: There were 4010 patients with an incident diagnosis of rAAA and 4010 age- and sex-matched AAA patients identified in the Danish National Registry of Patients. Data on comorbidity, concomitant drug use, primary health care utilization, socioeconomic status, and vital status were obtained from nationwide health care and administrative registries. RESULTS: Preadmission ASA use was identified for 1815 (45.3%) rAAA patients and 2111 (52.6%) AAA patients, corresponding to a crude odds ratio for rAAA in ASA users of 0.72 (95% confidence interval [CI], 0.66-0.79) compared with nonusers. However, after adjustment for possible confounders, no association between ASA use and the risk of rAAA was found (adjusted odds ratio, 0.97; 95% CI, 0.86-1.08). The aggregated 30-day rAAA case-fatality rate for users of ASA was 66.0% compared with 56.9% for nonusers, corresponding to an adjusted mortality rate ratio of 1.16 (95% CI, 1.06-1.27). CONCLUSIONS: Preadmission ASA use is not associated with an altered risk of AAA rupture but seems to be associated with a worse prognosis after rupture of AAA.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Wemmelund, Holger","Jørgensen, T.","Høgh, Annette","Behr-Rasmussen, Carsten","Johnsen, Søren Paaske","Lindholt, Jes S."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-258801"],"event":{"end":"2016-04-28","place":"Viborg, dk","start":"2016-04-28","title":"Forskningens Dag 2016","year":"2016"},"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"low dose aspirin and rupture ofabdominal aortic aneurysm a nationwide population based study","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dco","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-258801","ids":["au-e2d49614-229b-480a-8f9a-c8b572c9745c"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Local-1-1-C073535705F0F4FE4701772A11797D35"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Clinical Epidemiology","Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Karkirurgi, Viborg"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Miscellaneous","namevar":["Unspecified organisation"],"nora_code":"DI-OT","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"H.","key":"wemmelundpau","last":"Wemmelund","locid":"cb5ce986-d98a-4d46-9719-6da0669bf49c","name":"Wemmelund, Holger","name_org":"Wemmelund, H.","orcid":"0000-0002-5478-4889","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"T.","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","name":"Jørgensen, T.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"A.","key":"høghpau","last":"Høgh","locid":"78c4f7b4-e4f6-454b-8984-f1cdc83e4a82","name":"Høgh, Annette","name_org":"Høgh, A.","orcid":"0000-0001-7525-9007","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"C.","key":"behrrasmussenpau","last":"Behr-Rasmussen","locid":"a7e50d5c-fefe-45d9-ac73-7b46c0a3e724","name":"Behr-Rasmussen, Carsten","name_org":"Behr-Rasmussen, C.","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"S.P.","key":"johnsenpau","last":"Johnsen","locid":"4664f7a0-39e7-4f46-9121-1c3999eb3d09","name":"Johnsen, Søren Paaske","name_org":"Johnsen, S.P.","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"J.","key":"lindholtpau","last":"Lindholt","locid":"8c04d12e-9a82-4ded-8763-b9e82e31afd8","name":"Lindholt, Jes S.","name_org":"Lindholt, J.","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Miscellaneous","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Miscellaneous","Other Research","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-e2d49614-229b-480a-8f9a-c8b572c9745c"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Low-dose aspirin and rupture ofabdominal aortic aneurysm: A nationwide, population-based study.","type":"Conference Poster","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-258801","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Species attributes are commonly used to infer impacts of environmental change on multiyear species trends, e.g. decadal changes in population size. However, by themselves attributes are of limited value in global change attribution since they do not measure the changing environment. A broader foundation for attributing species responses to global change may be achieved by complementing an attributes-based approach by one estimating the relationship between repeated measures of organismal and environmental changes over short time scales. To assess the benefit of this multiscale perspective, we investigate the recent impact of multiple environmental changes on European farmland birds, here focusing on climate change and land use change. We analyze more than 800 time series from 18 countries spanning the past two decades. Analysis of long-term population growth rates documents simultaneous responses that can be attributed to both climate change and land-use change, including long-term increases in populations of hot-dwelling species and declines in long-distance migrants and farmland specialists. In contrast, analysis of annual growth rates yield novel insights into the potential mechanisms driving long-term climate induced change. In particular, we find that birds are affected by winter, spring, and summer conditions depending on the distinct breeding phenology that corresponds to their migratory strategy. Birds in general benefit from higher temperatures or higher primary productivity early on or in the peak of the breeding season with the largest effect sizes observed in cooler parts of species' climatic ranges. Our results document the potential of combining time scales and integrating both species attributes and environmental variables for global change attribution. We suggest such an approach will be of general use when high-resolution time series are available in large-scale biodiversity surveys.","author_count":17,"contributors":["Jørgensen, Peter Søgaard","Böhning-Gaese, Katrin","Thorup, Kasper","Tøttrup, Anders P.","Chylarecki, Przemysław","Jiguet, Frederic","Lehikoinen, Aleksi","Noble, David G.","Reif, Jiri","Schmid, Hans","van Turnhout, Chris","Burfield, Ian J.","Foppen, Ruud","Voříšek, Petr","van Strien, Arco","Gregory, Richard D.","Rahbek, Carsten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-418610"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Citizen science","Climate change","Farmland birds","Land use change"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"continent scale global change attribution in european birds combining annual and decadal time scales","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-418610","identifier":[{"id":"26486804","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84955709442","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-2a323424-1801-4acc-9f81-020aeee1de98"],"keyword":["Citizen science","Climate change","Farmland birds","Global change attribution","Land-use change","Multiple temporal scales","Multiscale inference","Population time series"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-4291E8801E8A109FF3F961E94E6324A0"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Science, Department of Biology, Ecology and Evolution"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Peter Søgaard","key":"jørgensenpau","last":"Jørgensen","locid":"f9ab4551-658e-4263-ac57-9399cf20f953","name":"Jørgensen, Peter Søgaard","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Katrin","key":"böhninggaesepau","last":"Böhning-Gaese","name":"Böhning-Gaese, Katrin","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Kasper","key":"thoruppau","last":"Thorup","locid":"d93b22de-bf1d-45f5-bcea-9dacd45b6420","name":"Thorup, Kasper","orcid":"0000-0002-0320-0601","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Anders P.","key":"tøttruppau","last":"Tøttrup","locid":"807731aa-098a-455e-bc69-e6413dc92e52","name":"Tøttrup, Anders P.","orcid":"0000-0001-8776-9629","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Przemysław","key":"chylareckipau","last":"Chylarecki","name":"Chylarecki, Przemysław","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Frederic","key":"jiguetpau","last":"Jiguet","name":"Jiguet, Frederic","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Aleksi","key":"lehikoinenpau","last":"Lehikoinen","name":"Lehikoinen, Aleksi","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"David G.","key":"noblepau","last":"Noble","name":"Noble, David G.","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jiri","key":"reifpau","last":"Reif","name":"Reif, Jiri","rank":9,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Hans","key":"schmidpau","last":"Schmid","name":"Schmid, Hans","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Chris","key":"vanturnhoutpau","last":"van Turnhout","name":"van Turnhout, Chris","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Ian J.","key":"burfieldpau","last":"Burfield","name":"Burfield, Ian J.","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Ruud","key":"foppenpau","last":"Foppen","name":"Foppen, Ruud","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Petr","key":"voříšekpau","last":"Voříšek","name":"Voříšek, Petr","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Arco","key":"vanstrienpau","last":"van Strien","name":"van Strien, Arco","rank":15,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Richard D.","key":"gregorypau","last":"Gregory","name":"Gregory, Richard D.","rank":16,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Carsten","key":"rahbekpau","last":"Rahbek","locid":"7aab0090-fb0d-4af4-8c65-8e6edbef6684","name":"Rahbek, Carsten","orcid":"0000-0003-4585-0300","rank":17,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26486804"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/gcb.13097","issn":["13541013","13652486"],"issn_org":["13541013"],"issue":"2","journal":"Global Change Biology","pages":"530-543","status":"p","title":"Global Change Biology","volume":"22"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84955709442"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-2a323424-1801-4acc-9f81-020aeee1de98"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Continent-scale global change attribution in European birds - combining annual and decadal time scales","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-418610","year":"2016","yearsub":"2016"}
{"abstract":"Postprandial hyperinsulinemia characterizes Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (RYGB) and sometimes leads to reactive hypoglycemia. We prospectively evaluated changes in beta cell function in seven RYGB-operated patients with a median follow-up of 2.9 years with hyperglycemic clamps and oral glucose tolerance tests (OGTTs). Three years after RYGB, weight loss was 26 % and insulin sensitivity had improved. Insulin secretion during clamp experiments was largely unchanged compared to before surgery. In contrast, insulin secretion in response to the OGTTs doubled when evaluated by the disposition index and 2-h plasma glucose declined to a mean of 3.3 ± 0.3 mmol/l postoperatively. Our findings indicate that intrinsic beta cell function remains unchanged in glucose-tolerant patients even years after RYGB, while altered gut-islet regulation drive risk of postprandial hyperinsulinemic hypoglycemia.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Dirksen, Carsten","Eiken, Aleksander","Bojsen-Møller, Kirstine N","Svane, Maria S.","Martinussen, Christoffer","Jørgensen, Nils B","Holst, Jens Juul","Madsbad, Sten"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-530036"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Journal Article"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"no islet cell hyperfunction but altered gut islet regulation and postprandial hypoglycemia in glucose tolerant patients 000000000003 years after gastric bypass surgery","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-530036","identifier":[{"id":"84965019179","type":"scopus"},{"id":"27138601","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-e4cbeb61-e5f9-472f-a19f-63a25f6ebf90"],"keyword":["Journal Article"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Glob