{"abstract":"Recent studies suggest that exposure to perfluorinated alkylate substances (PFASs) may induce immunosuppression in humans and animal models. In this exploratory study, 12 healthy adult volunteers were recruited. With each subject, serum-PFAS concentrations were measured and their antibody responses prospectively followed for 30 days after a booster vaccination with diphtheria and tetanus. The results indicated that serum-PFAS concentrations were positively correlated and positively associated with age and male sex. The specific antibody concentrations in serum were increased from Day 4 to Day 10 post-booster, after which a constant concentration was reached. Serum PFAS concentrations showed significant negative associations with the rate of increase in the antibody responses. Interestingly, this effect was particularly strong for the longer-chain PFASs. All significant associations remained significant after adjustment for sex and age. Although the study involved a small number of subjects, these findings of a PFAS-associated reduction of the early humoral immune response to booster vaccination in healthy adults supported previous findings of PFAS immunosuppression in larger cohorts. Furthermore, the results suggested that cellular mechanisms right after antigen exposure should be investigated more closely to identify possible mechanisms of immunosuppression from PFAS.","author_count":7,"contributors":["Kielsen, Katrine","Shamim, Zaiba","Ryder, Lars P.","Nielsen, Flemming","Grandjean, Philippe","Budtz-Jørgensen, Esben","Heilmann, Carsten"],"cuid":["S02-095.83-043979"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"antibody response to booster vaccination with tetanus and diphtheria in adults exposed to perfluorinated alkylates","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4739630/pdf/nihms753623.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},"id":"S02-095.83-043979","identifier":[{"id":"84953337691","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC4739630","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"26181512","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-82bf9dc0-6bfa-4c7e-a7fb-2516cf8ad4ba","sdu-5d6b893a-8bd8-498e-a4de-689f26247a1b"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-0071629FD742F1AAC3240901DD433F2D"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4739630/pdf/nihms753623.pdf","version":"Pre-print"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3109/1547691X.2015.1067259","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Public Health, Environmental Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"us","key":"harvard t h chan school of public health","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Public Health, Section of Biostatistics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Katrine","key":"kielsenpau","last":"Kielsen","name":"Kielsen, Katrine","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Zaiba","key":"shamimpau","last":"Shamim","name":"Shamim, Zaiba","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars P","key":"ryderpau","last":"Ryder","name":"Ryder, Lars P","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Flemming","key":"nielsenpau","last":"Nielsen","locid":"a3417ee2-84f4-4f19-ab48-919e04e98666","name":"Nielsen, Flemming","orcid":"0000-0002-5657-405X","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Philippe","key":"grandjeanpau","last":"Grandjean","locid":"6fe207c8-8ce6-41b9-8503-bc15b6cf3ea1","name":"Grandjean, Philippe","orcid":"0000-0003-4046-9658","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"4","first":"Esben","key":"budtzjørgensenpau","last":"Budtz-Jørgensen","locid":"c4ebc45c-df67-437e-a5e9-bdaa629d7caa","name":"Budtz-Jørgensen, Esben","orcid":"0000-0002-5551-0724","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Carsten","key":"heilmannpau","last":"Heilmann","name":"Heilmann, Carsten","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["26181512"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3109/1547691x.2015.1067259","issn":["15476901","1547691X"],"issn_org":["1547691X"],"issue":"2","journal":"Journal of Immunotoxicology","pages":"270-273","publisher":"Informa Healthcare","status":"p","title":"Journal of Immunotoxicology","volume":"13"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84953337691"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-82bf9dc0-6bfa-4c7e-a7fb-2516cf8ad4ba","sdu-5d6b893a-8bd8-498e-a4de-689f26247a1b"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Antibody response to booster vaccination with tetanus and diphtheria in adults exposed to perfluorinated alkylates","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-095.83-043979","year":2016,"yearsub":2016}
{"abstract":"Background: Roux-en-Y gastric bypass (RYGB) is an effective means to achieve sustained weight loss for morbidly obese individuals. Besides rapid weight reduction, patients achieve major improvements of insulin sensitivity and glucose homeostasis. Dysbiosis of gut microbiota has been associated with obesity and some of its co-morbidities, like type 2 diabetes, and major changes of gut microbial communities have been hypothesized to mediate part of the beneficial metabolic effects observed after RYGB. Here we describe changes in gut microbial taxonomic composition and functional potential following RYGB. Methods: We recruited 13 morbidly obese patients who underwent RYGB, carefully phenotyped them, and had their gut microbiomes quantified before (n = 13) and 3 months (n = 12) and 12 months (n = 8) after RYGB. Following shotgun metagenomic sequencing of the fecal microbial DNA purified from stools, we characterized the gut microbial composition at species and gene levels followed by functional annotation. Results: In parallel with the weight loss and metabolic improvements, gut microbial diversity increased within the first 3 months after RYGB and remained high 1 year later. RYGB led to altered relative abundances of 31 species (P < 0.05, q < 0.15) within the first 3 months, including those of Escherichia coli, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Veillonella spp., Streptococcus spp., Alistipes spp., and Akkermansia muciniphila. Sixteen of these species maintained their altered relative abundances during the following 9 months. Interestingly, Faecalibacterium prausnitzii was the only species that decreased in relative abundance. Fifty-three microbial functional modules increased their relative abundance between baseline and 3 months (P < 0.05, q < 0.17). These functional changes included increased potential (i) to assimilate multiple energy sources using transporters and phosphotransferase systems, (ii) to use aerobic respiration, (iii) to shift from protein degradation to putrefaction, and (iv) to use amino acids and fatty acids as energy sources. Conclusions: Within 3 months after morbidly obese individuals had undergone RYGB, their gut microbiota featured an increased diversity, an altered composition, an increased potential for oxygen tolerance, and an increased potential for microbial utilization of macro- and micro-nutrients. These changes were maintained for the first year post-RYGB. Trial registration: Current controlled trials (ID NCT00810823 , NCT01579981 , and NCT01993511 ).","author_count":19,"contributors":["Palleja, Albert","Kashani, Alireza","Allin, Kristine H.","Nielsen, Trine","Zhang, Chenchen","Li, Yin","Brach, Thorsten","Liang, Suisha","Feng, Qiang","Jørgensen, Nils Bruun","Bojsen-Møller, Kirstine N.","Dirksen, Carsten","Burgdorf, Kristoffer Sølvsten","Holst, Jens Juul","Madsbad, Sten","Wang, Jun","Pedersen, Oluf","Hansen, Torben","Arumugam, Manimozhiyan"],"cuid":["S02-099.70-042368"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, East"],"country":["China"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","BGI Group","China Agricultural University","iCarbonX"],"keyword":["Anastomosis Roux en Y","Bacteria classification","Biodiversity","Feces microbiology","Female","Gastrointestinal Microbiome","Humans","Longitudinal Studies","Male","Metagenomics","Sequence Analysis DNA","Treatment Outcome","Weight loss"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"roux en y gastric bypass surgery of morbidly obese patients induces swift and persistent changes of the individual gut microbiota","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13073-016-0312-1","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-099.70-042368","identifier":[{"id":"PMC4908688","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"27306058","type":"pmid"},{"id":"000378592500001","type":"wos"},{"id":"84974587962","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["ku-509c23f3-cc89-4b29-808a-306ed130f5f5","sdu-deea3584-611c-47a7-b78b-1c8e1501febc"],"keyword":["Anastomosis, Roux-en-Y","Bacteria/classification","Biodiversity","Feces/microbiology","Female","Gastrointestinal Microbiome","Humans","Longitudinal Studies","Male","Metagenomics","Obesity, Morbid/microbiology","Sequence Analysis, DNA","Treatment Outcome","Weight Loss"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-A96ED5F0E16009EE0EC31D28E234E225"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/124266971/Roux_en_Y_gastric_bypass_surgery_of_morbidly_obese_patients_induces_swift_and_persistent_changes_of_the_individual_gut_microbiota.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1186/s13073-016-0312-1","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/164595321/Roux_en_Y_gastric_bypass_surgery_of_morbidly_obese_patients_induces_swift_and_persistent_changes_of_the_individual_gut_microbiota.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2016","yearSub":"2016"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Protein Research, Disease Systems Biology Program"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affn